m.", 139.3, 139.66], ["Up", 139.86, 140.0], ["to", 140.0, 140.18], ["65", 140.18, 140.7], ["%", 140.7, 141.16], ["off", 141.16, 141.58], ["right", 141.58, 141.92], ["now.", 141.92, 142.26], ["With", 142.58, 142.74], ["a", 142.74, 142.84], ["90", 142.84, 143.16], ["-day", 143.16, 143.48], ["money", 143.48, 143.76], ["-back", 143.76, 144.04], ["guarantee,", 144.04, 144.62], ["tap", 145.0, 145.14], ["below.", 145.14, 145.56]], "segments": [[0.0, 5.24, "Here's what happens to your airways if you slide two capture cards under your mattress and pillow"], [5.24, 9.9, "and leave them there for four weeks. Okay, this is the part nobody's talking about."], [10.26, 15.54, "Dust mites can directly damage your airway cells through mitochondrial injury and abnormal"], [15.54, 21.26, "autophagy. In plain English, the cells lining your airways can lose their power and stop"], [21.26, 26.24, "cleaning themselves up properly. So here's what happens to your airways if you slide two"], [26.24, 30.66, "capture cards under your mattress and pillow and leave them there for four weeks."], [31.04, 36.04, "Slide them in. That's the whole job. The first night while you sleep, the lure starts drawing"], [36.04, 41.38, "dust mites up out of the foam. The ones building up two inches under your face. That's the plant"], [41.38, 46.1, "derived pheromone lure, pulling them away from the place you breathe for eight hours a night."], [46.34, 51.06, "By the end of week one, most people find they're not reaching for a tissue the second they sit"], [51.06, 56.62, "up. That's because every mite the lure pulls out stops dropping its twenty pellets a day of"], [56.62, 61.5, "derpy one where your face sleeps. The allergen that breaks down the junctions holding your"], [61.5, 66.02, "airway lining together. Week two, you stop wondering if you're coming down with something"], [66.02, 70.84, "and that clearer by lunch feeling starts showing up at breakfast. That's the adhesive,"], [71.24, 75.94, "locking every mite the lure pulls in. So what it catches comes out of your bed"], [75.94, 80.14, "instead of back into it. The craziest part? You haven't sprayed a thing."], [80.14, 85.04, "You haven't stripped the bed. You haven't bought a new mattress. But every single night,"], [85.32, 90.28, "the mattress you sleep on is giving them up. Week four, by now, most people are sleeping"], [90.28, 94.94, "through more and waking up less blocked. Mornings start the way they did years ago."], [95.24, 99.46, "And that background dread before you even open your eyes is finally gone"], [99.46, 104.78, "for you and for whoever shares your bed. How is this possible? Most mite products"], [104.78, 110.52, "try to kill them. But every dead one stays in the bed, carrying the same allergen as a live one."], [110.82, 115.98, "This one pulls them out alive. Dust mites have no eyes. They find each other by scent,"], [116.28, 121.16, "a chemical signal. Capture cards mimic that signal. Drawing them up out of the foam"], [121.16, 126.36, "and the adhesive locks them on. It's lure and trap. The way pest control has caught"], [126.36, 131.4, "things for decades aimed at the one place nobody had. The inside of a mattress."], [131.4, 137.16, "Because a dead mite can't walk to a card, over 30,000 homes are already pulling them"], [137.16, 142.26, "out of their beds instead of washing around them. Up to 65% off right now."], [142.58, 145.56, "With a 90-day money-back guarantee, tap below."]]}